12:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L19",null,{"guide":{"date":"Spring 1864","place":"Virginia theater: the Rapidan River, the Wilderness, Richmond, Bermuda Hundred, and the Shenandoah Valley","result":"Grant made Lee’s army the main U.S. objective in Virginia and paired the Army of the Potomac’s movement around Lee’s right with supporting offensives by Benjamin Butler and Franz Sigel. Meade’s staff planned to cross east of Lee and move through the Wilderness in one day before the Confederates could interfere. The Army of the Potomac and IX Corps fielded more than 116,000 men, compared with fewer than 64,000 in Lee’s army, but the U.S. force contained many inexperienced soldiers and short-term veterans. Lee correctly anticipated most of Grant’s Virginia plan but underestimated the size of the main army","facts":[{"label":"Army of the Potomac","value":"More than 100,000 men"},{"label":"Burnside’s IX Corps","value":"About 19,200 men"},{"label":"Army of Northern Virginia","value":"Fewer than 64,000 men"},{"label":"Lee’s estimate of Meade","value":"About 75,000; roughly 36% low"},{"label":"Wilderness area","value":"About 70 square miles"},{"label":"Butler’s starting force","value":"About 21,500 plus 10,000 reinforcements"},{"label":"Bermuda Hundred to Richmond","value":"About 16 miles"},{"label":"Bermuda Hundred to Petersburg","value":"About 8 miles"}],"maps":[{"title":"Virginia operations planned for 1864","caption":"Grant’s main army would cross the Rapidan east of Lee, Butler would move up the James to Bermuda Hundred, and Sigel would advance through the Shenandoah Valley and against the Virginia and Tennessee Railroad.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-80.20%2C36.50%2C-75.60%2C39.60&layer=mapnik&marker=38.3227%2C-77.7564"},{"title":"The Rapidan–Richmond corridor","caption":"The Overland Campaign map shows the rivers crossing the north–south route, the Wilderness east of Lee’s camps near Orange, and the succession of positions between the Rapidan and Richmond.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/","image":"/images/overland-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of the Overland Campaign corridor from the Rapidan River to Richmond"},{"title":"The opening battlefield","caption":"The first NPS movement map shows the limited road network inside the Wilderness: Orange Turnpike, Orange Plank Road, Germanna Plank Road, and Brock Road.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=F6B164DB-01BE-4E4E-A97CC65D8F0FD22E&id=17fa8c32-a2c7-4633-92e2-d76770d6fee4","image":"/images/wilderness-may5-noon-3pm-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed National Park Service map of the roads and terrain at the Battle of the Wilderness"}],"moments":[{"time":"2:29","audioTime":149,"title":"Grant planned simultaneous offensives","detail":"The 1864 strategy called for pressure in several theaters so Confederate armies could not reinforce one another."},{"time":"3:00","audioTime":180,"title":"Lee’s army was the main eastern objective","detail":"Political and military considerations required the principal U.S. effort in Virginia to target the Army of Northern Virginia."},{"time":"3:47","audioTime":227,"title":"Virginia’s geography restricted movement","detail":"Chesapeake Bay, the mountains, and west-to-east rivers limited north–south routes and repeatedly favored defenders."},{"time":"4:34","audioTime":274,"title":"U.S. sea power created another approach","detail":"Federal control of the coast made it possible to land and supply troops southeast of Richmond instead of crossing every Virginia river overland."},{"time":"6:24","audioTime":384,"title":"Grant assigned forces in southeastern Virginia","detail":"About 21,500 troops already defended Hampton Roads, and Grant added roughly 10,000 men from X Corps for offensive use."},{"time":"7:19","audioTime":439,"title":"Grant met Benjamin Butler","detail":"At Fort Monroe, Butler proposed moving by water up the James River and landing at Bermuda Hundred."},{"time":"8:02","audioTime":482,"title":"Bermuda Hundred threatened two cities","detail":"The peninsula lay near the Richmond–Petersburg railroad, about 16 miles from Richmond and eight miles from Petersburg."},{"time":"9:01","audioTime":541,"title":"Grant considered the main Rapidan movement","detail":"Lee’s army camped between Gordonsville and Orange Court House, covered the Rapidan fords, and protected important rail and Shenandoah Valley connections."},{"time":"10:02","audioTime":602,"title":"Grant rejected a frontal river attack","detail":"The fortified Rapidan crossings were too strong. A movement around Lee’s western flank would also create serious supply problems."},{"time":"10:36","audioTime":636,"title":"Grant chose Lee’s eastern flank","detail":"Crossing below Lee would be easier to supply and would keep open the possibility of cooperation with Butler."},{"time":"10:53","audioTime":653,"title":"Meade retained operational command","detail":"Grant accompanied the Army of the Potomac but left its day-to-day management to George Meade."},{"time":"12:08","audioTime":728,"title":"Andrew Humphreys designed the movement","detail":"Meade’s chief of staff planned the detailed turn around Lee’s right."},{"time":"12:18","audioTime":738,"title":"The Wilderness was the main obstacle","detail":"Roughly 70 square miles of dense second-growth vegetation and few roads made organized movement difficult and favored defense."},{"time":"13:37","audioTime":817,"title":"The plan required a one-day passage","detail":"The Army of the Potomac was to cross the Rapidan and clear the Wilderness before Lee could intervene."},{"time":"13:49","audioTime":829,"title":"Humphreys planned to turn Mine Run","detail":"A wide movement toward the stream’s headwaters would make Lee’s fortified Mine Run position unusable and force him to retreat or fight in open country."},{"time":"14:49","audioTime":889,"title":"Grant added the Shenandoah offensive","detail":"Franz Sigel’s forces would move up the valley while other columns attacked the Virginia and Tennessee Railroad, lead mines, and saltworks."},{"time":"16:25","audioTime":985,"title":"The presidential election affected strategy","detail":"A major U.S. defeat could increase support for a negotiated peace, while battlefield success could strengthen Lincoln’s reelection."},{"time":"18:08","audioTime":1088,"title":"Longstreet proposed a political offensive","detail":"He suggested a Confederate movement into Kentucky to damage Northern morale and Lincoln’s electoral prospects, but practical objections ended the plan."},{"time":"20:31","audioTime":1231,"title":"Lee faced desertion and shortages","detail":"Absences reduced Confederate strength, while inadequate food and forage threatened the army’s ability to respond quickly."},{"time":"22:37","audioTime":1357,"title":"Lee operated his own intelligence system","detail":"Reports came through Stuart, Richmond officials, scouts, civilians, and newspapers; Lee had no formal chief of intelligence."},{"time":"23:28","audioTime":1408,"title":"Lee anticipated the supporting offensives","detail":"By April 30, he expected operations in the Shenandoah Valley, against southwestern railroads, and from southeastern Virginia."},{"time":"24:09","audioTime":1449,"title":"Lee underestimated Meade’s strength","detail":"He estimated 75,000 U.S. troops. The Army of the Potomac already exceeded 100,000 and Burnside would add more than 16,000."},{"time":"24:58","audioTime":1498,"title":"Lee fielded three infantry corps","detail":"Longstreet had about 10,000 men, Ewell about 17,200, and A. P. Hill just over 22,300, plus Stuart’s cavalry."},{"time":"26:28","audioTime":1588,"title":"Meade also fielded three infantry corps","detail":"Sedgwick’s VI Corps had about 24,000, Hancock’s II Corps about 26,600, and Warren’s V Corps about 24,100."},{"time":"28:15","audioTime":1695,"title":"Sheridan commanded about 15,800 cavalry","detail":"Grant brought him east from infantry command in the Western Theater to lead the Army of the Potomac’s three cavalry divisions."},{"time":"29:14","audioTime":1754,"title":"Burnside remained administratively separate","detail":"IX Corps had about 19,200 men and reported directly to Grant because Burnside outranked Meade."},{"time":"30:14","audioTime":1814,"title":"The U.S. numerical advantage had limits","detail":"Up to one-third of Meade’s soldiers lacked combat experience, and some veteran enlistments were close to expiring."},{"time":"33:27","audioTime":2007,"title":"About 27,000 veterans reenlisted","detail":"Federal bonuses and furloughs retained many experienced soldiers, but thousands of others were due to leave during the campaign."},{"time":"34:34","audioTime":2074,"title":"The U.S. army still had about 70,000 veterans","detail":"The Army of the Potomac had roughly 65,000 experienced troops; IX Corps veterans raised the combined total slightly above 70,000."},{"time":"34:58","audioTime":2098,"title":"The Army of the Potomac was well supplied","detail":"Morale and material condition were strong, with adequate clothing, equipment, food, and an effective logistical system."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"General-in-chief","subordinates":"Coordinated Meade, Butler, and Sigel across Virginia"},{"commander":"George G. Meade","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"John Sedgwick’s VI Corps, Winfield Scott Hancock’s II Corps, Gouverneur K. Warren’s V Corps, and Philip Sheridan’s cavalry"},{"commander":"Ambrose E. Burnside","command":"IX Corps","subordinates":"Four divisions reporting directly to Grant at the campaign’s start"},{"commander":"Benjamin F. Butler","command":"Department of Virginia and North Carolina","subordinates":"Army of the James movement to Bermuda Hundred"},{"commander":"Franz Sigel","command":"Department of West Virginia","subordinates":"Shenandoah Valley and southwestern Virginia operations"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Personally evaluated intelligence and directed the army’s response"},{"commander":"James Longstreet","command":"First Corps","subordinates":"Two divisions, about 10,000 men"},{"commander":"Richard S. Ewell","command":"Second Corps","subordinates":"Three divisions, about 17,200 men"},{"commander":"A. P. Hill","command":"Third Corps","subordinates":"Three divisions, just over 22,300 men"},{"commander":"J. E. B. Stuart","command":"Cavalry Corps","subordinates":"Cavalry scouting and intelligence collection"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Andrew A. Humphreys","role":"Designed the Army of the Potomac’s planned movement around Lee’s right flank","side":"United States"},{"name":"Benjamin F. Butler","role":"Planned the waterborne landing at Bermuda Hundred","side":"United States"},{"name":"Franz Sigel","role":"Commanded the supporting Shenandoah Valley offensive","side":"United States"},{"name":"George G. Meade","role":"Retained operational command of the Army of the Potomac under Grant","side":"United States"},{"name":"James Longstreet","role":"Commanded Lee’s First Corps and had proposed a politically aimed offensive into Kentucky","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"J. E. B. Stuart","role":"Commanded Confederate cavalry and managed much of Lee’s intelligence network","side":"Confederacy"},{"personId":"ulysses-s-grant","role":"Set the coordinated Virginia strategy and chose the eastern approach around Lee"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Commanded the Army of Northern Virginia and anticipated most of Grant’s plan","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/robert-e-lee-1864.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666540/"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/overland-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of the Overland Campaign","caption":"The rivers, roads, and narrow corridor between Washington and Richmond shaped the campaign plan.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/"},{"image":"/images/ulysses-s-grant.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Ulysses S. Grant","caption":"Grant coordinated three Virginia offensives while leaving day-to-day control of the Army of the Potomac to Meade.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666428/"},{"image":"/images/robert-e-lee-1864.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Robert E. Lee","caption":"Lee correctly anticipated most of the U.S. operational plan but underestimated the main army’s size.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666540/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 479","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2025/01/05/civil-war-podcast-episode-479/"},{"label":"National Park Service: History of the Battle of the Wilderness","url":"https://www.nps.gov/frsp/learn/historyculture/wildspot.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Wilderness troop movement maps","url":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery.htm?id=F6B164DB-01BE-4E4E-A97CC65D8F0FD22E"},{"label":"National Park Service: Port Walthall Junction and the Bermuda Hundred Campaign","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va047"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Overland Campaign map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/"}]}}]}]
